'''wasten†''' (wās′ten), ''n.'' [< ME. ''wastine'', ''wasteyn'', < OF. ''wastine'', ''guastine'', waste, desert (cf. AS. ''wēsten'' = OS. ''wōstun'' = OHG. ''wuosti'', a desert, waste, wilderness): see ''waste''<sup>1</sup>.] A waste; a desert.
{{smaller block|<poem>A gode man and ryȝt certeyn
Dwelled besyde that ''wasteyn''.</poem>
{{right|''MS. Harl.'' 1701, f. 12. (''Halliwell.'')}}
<poem>She, of nought affrayd,
Through woods and ''wastnes'' wide him daily sought.</poem>
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., I. iii. 3.}}}}

'''wasteness''' (wāst′nes), ''n.'' The state of being waste or desolate; desolation.
{{smaller block|That day is a day of wrath, a day of trouble and distress, a day of ''wasteness''. {{right|Zeph. i. 15.}}}}

'''waste-pallet''' (wāst′pal″et), ''n.'' See ''pallet''<sup>2</sup>, 5.

'''waste-picker''' (wāst′pik″ėr), ''n.'' Same as ''rag-picker'', 1.

'''waste-pipe''' (wāst′pīp), ''n.'' A pipe for conveying away waste water, etc.; an overflow-pipe. See ''waste-steam pipe'', under ''waste''<sup>1</sup>, ''a.''

'''waste-preventer''' (wāst′prē-ven″tėr), ''n.'' In ''plumbing'', a device for controlling the supply and flow of a water-tank. {{smaller|It combines an outlet-valve and a ball-valve on the inlet-pipe—a single lever operated by a chain so controlling both valves that no more water enters the tank than is drawn out.}}

